    the reflection-scale cycle is a multiplicative holonomy, the viscous history is a coboundary, and the memory kernel is positive
    
    प्रतिबिम्ब-भ्रमण. VyarthaCakra put up the additive fence; this is the same
    fence multiplicatively, where the defect is a closed transport rather
    than a difference. On one reflected pair, with the swap as reflection
    and a unit pair acting diagonally:
    
      §1 swap ∘ act(u,v) ∘ swap ∘ act(u⁻¹,v⁻¹) ≡ act (v·u⁻¹ , u·v⁻¹).
         The holonomy is computed, not posited.
      §2 hol ≡ (1,1) ⟺ u ≡ v, both directions.
      §3 swap(hol) · hol ≡ (1,1) for EVERY unit pair — so that relation is
         automatic and separates nothing; §2 is the one with content.
      §4 hol(w·w') ≡ hol w · hol w', so one nontrivial value forces all.
      §5 (x² + y²) - 2 ≡ (x - y)² whenever x·y ≡ 1: the two-sided scale
         probe minus twice the centre is a square, with no hypothesis
         beyond invertibility, so it is midpoint convex exactly.
      §6 and its vanishing forces x·x ≡ 1r.
    
    §6 gives x² ≡ 1, NOT x ≡ 1 — and that single gap is positivity of the
    scale factor. It is now located precisely: everything else in the
    circuit is ring algebra and is proved.
    
    काल-सेतु. The step law ν·S n ≡ (P n - P (n+1)) + N n telescopes:
    
      ν · Σ_{n<k} S n ≡ (P 0 - P k) + Σ_{n<k} N n,
    
    and on a loop (P k ≡ P 0) the endpoint term is not small, it is ABSENT:
    ν·(strain circulation) ≡ (residual circulation). No cohomology class
    needs inventing — §3 exhibits the primitive. The index is a partition
    and the identity is exact at every partition, so nothing is lost to
    discretisation; a continuum version changes the carrier, not the
    content. ν is never divided through.
    
    स्मृति-बीज. The memory kernel's strict positivity — the reason a
    nonnegative shell profile has strictly positive memory — is one
    inequality between two cubes, and it holds in ℕ:
    
      0 < t → s ≤ t → 2·s³ < 3·t³,
    
    so the bracket never vanishes and never turns; at s ≡ t it is still
    t³ > 0. Its ratio bound is exact in subtraction-free form: with
    2·s³ + d ≡ 3·t³, s·d ≤ s·(3·t³), discarding nothing. And SesaDvaya's
    truncated inverse IS the closed form of the tower's sum: whenever
    (1-x)² has an inverse c, Σ_{j≤N}(j+1)xʲ ≡ c·(1 - (N+2)x^{N+1} +
    (N+1)x^{N+2}) — no numerals, no division, no convergence.
    
    All three checked --safe at the pin, no postulates, no holes.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SK5zsbQkVFLWgjaSk4NpiQ
